  {
    "date": "2021-12-13",
    "term": "終える",
    "kana": "おえる",
    "meaning": "to finish",
    "level": "N3",
    "pos": ["Ichidan verb", "transitive verb", "auxiliary verb"],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["sound-change"],
    "headline": "終える traces a long sound path from Old Japanese wopu, through /wofuru/ and /oheru/, to /oeru/.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "/wopu/ → (attributive) /wopuru/ → /wofuru/ → (grammatical shift) /woferu/ → /oheru/ → /oeru/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese verb wopu, from Proto-Japonic *wopu."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Development"
      },
      {
        "quote": "Natural progression from Old and Classical Japanese 下(しも)二(に)段(だん)活(かつ)用(よう) (shimo nidan katsuyō, “lower bigrade conjugation”) verbs ending in -u to modern Japanese 下(しも)一(いち)段(だん)活(かつ)用(よう) (shimo ichidan katsuyō, “lower monograde conjugation”) verbs ending in -eru."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The root verb was ambitransitive, with both transitive and intransitive senses conjugated using the 下二段活用 (shimo nidan katsuyō, “lower bigrade conjugation”) pattern. Over time, the two senses differentiated as the intransitive evolved into modern 終わる (owaru, also derivable as the spontaneous or passive -aru ending from a putative but unrecorded 四段活用 (yodan katsuyō), the full quadrigrade conjugation pattern), while the transitive evolved into modern 終える (oeru, with the 下一段活用 (shimo ichidan katsuyō, “lower monograde conjugation”) pattern, also known as \"type 2\", and the expected historical development for shimo nidan verbs)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Probably not related to superficially similar 追う (ou, ancient opu, “to follow”, quadrigrade conjugation in older Japanese), considering the distinct lack of initial /w/ in ancient spellings."
      }
    ],
    "wiktionaryDump": "2026-09-02"
  },
